THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001 defines fragility as 'distance to the nearest graph in which a committed root is defeated' and its own criticism admits the graph-distance metric is non-canonical. This is a substance fault under the stated problem: the fixed-point extension over a graph sequence is only deterministic relative to an unspecified distance, so the proposal does not preserve determinism as an intrinsic guarantee — it silently externalizes determinism to an undefined metric choice. Different admissible distance functions yield different nearest-neighbor graphs and thus different committed-root defeat conditions for the same input, so the limit semantics is not determined by the attack graph alone.

TARGET:
Introduce a temporal dimension: define the grounded extension over a sequence of attack graphs where each step adds or removes edges, and require that the extension is the unique limit of the monotone fixed-point iterations over the sequence. This makes change first-class and allows a semantic definition of 'fragility' as the distance to the nearest graph in which a committed root is defeated; the cost is polynomial because each step uses the previous label as a warm start and the operator is monotone. This makes incremental caching a semantic, not merely an implementation, property. Criticism: the definition of distance between graphs is non-canonical and may affect the resulting extension.